---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { commons } from '../config/commons';

const crumbs = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Contribute' }];

const NEVER = [
	'A document of any kind. There is no upload button and there will not be one — a declarations page is the densest packet of personal information a person owns, and asking for one would turn a research property into a data-breach liability.',
	'A policy number or a claim number.',
	'A date of birth, a social security number, or any government identifier.',
	'Health information.',
	'A payment method. Nothing here is for sale.',
	'Your address, beyond the state the situation happened in.',
];

const COLLECTED = [
	'An email address, used to sign in and to ask you questions about your account before it publishes. Nothing else.',
	'A display name, which is what appears on anything you contribute.',
	'For practitioners only: a professional licence number, checked against the regulator’s own public register.',
];
---

<BaseLayout
	title="What contributing will involve"
	eyebrow="Contribute"
	lede="Accounts are not open yet. This is what the route in will ask for, and — more importantly — what it will never ask for."
	crumbs={crumbs}
>
	<section class="band band-tight">
		<div class="shell page-grid">
			<div class="prose">
				<div class="callout" data-tone="warn">
					<h3>Not open yet</h3>
					<p>
						There is no sign-in on this site today and no way to submit anything. This page is
						published ahead of the mechanism so that what it will and will not ask for is on the
						record before anyone is asked to trust it.
					</p>
				</div>

				<h2>Two ways to contribute, in this order</h2>
				<h3>1. A case report</h3>
				<p>
					Your own account of a real insurance situation: what happened, when, in which state, on
					which kind of policy, and who decided. It is moderated, labelled and edited for
					identifying detail before it publishes, and you can have it withdrawn at any time.
				</p>
				<p>
					What it is not is a place to ask whether your claim should have been paid. Nobody here
					answers that — <a href="/standards">and nobody is permitted to</a>.
				</p>

				<h3>2. A practitioner note</h3>
				<p>
					If you hold a professional licence — broker, adjuster, attorney — you can attach a signed,
					named note to a specific claim on{' '}
					<a href={commons.record.origin} rel="noopener">{commons.record.name}</a>: a sentence of
					law, with your reading of how it plays out in practice beside it.
				</p>
				<p>
					Verification is a licence number checked against the regulator's own public register.
					Because that register is public, verifying you needs <strong>no identity documents and
					stores nothing sensitive</strong>. That is precisely why practitioner notes come before
					verified policyholder status: cheaper to verify, and worth more.
				</p>

				<h2>What is collected</h2>
				<ul>{COLLECTED.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}</ul>

				<h2>What is never collected</h2>
				<ul>{NEVER.map((n) => <li>{n}</li>)}</ul>
				<p>
					These are not settings. The fields do not exist in the data model, which is the only
					version of this promise worth making: a field that was never built cannot be quietly
					filled in by a later form.
				</p>

				<h2>Standing, and how it is earned</h2>
				<p>
					There are no points, no streaks, no upvotes and no reply counts. A system that rewards
					answering questions creates an incentive to give coverage advice, which is the one thing
					that must not happen here. Nothing on this site rewards being first to reply.
				</p>
				<p>Standing comes from four things:</p>
				<ul>
					<li>A verified professional licence, shown with its number and the register it was checked against.</li>
					<li>Reports published after moderation.</li>
					<li>Annotations that a Record page links to.</li>
					<li>Corrections accepted — worth more than anything else on this list.</li>
				</ul>

				<h2>Open discussion</h2>
				<p>
					Threads come last, and only once the two mechanisms above are worth reading. When they
					open they will be for what changed and what it means — a bill moving, a regulation
					adopted, a form edition superseded, a carrier withdrawing from a county — because that is
					where public discussion is genuinely additive. The Record can say what the rule is; it
					cannot say what practitioners are seeing this month.
				</p>
			</div>

			<aside aria-label="Summary">
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Collected</h2>
					<ul>
						<li>Email, to sign in</li>
						<li>A display name</li>
						<li>A licence number, if you have one</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Never collected</h2>
					<ul>
						<li>Any document upload</li>
						<li>Policy or claim numbers</li>
						<li>Date of birth or government id</li>
						<li>Health information</li>
						<li>A payment method</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
				<div class="rail-block">
					<h2>Related</h2>
					<ul>
						<li><a href="/standards">Standards</a></li>
						<li><a href="/moderation">Moderation</a></li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</aside>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
